# Agent worktrees and scratch
.claude/worktrees/

# Eval run output: transcripts, per-case results and the spend of one pass.
# Regenerated by `worker/eval/run.ts` and specific to whoever ran it.
#
# Any `ref/` is the exception and is tracked, because it is not output — it is
# a frozen opponent that later runs are judged against. Regenerating one would
# silently change what a win rate is a rate of, so they are committed and
# reviewed like any other fixture.
#
# Any, not just `baseline/`: a change to what is asked of the model rather than
# to the code — a different model, a different reasoning effort — cannot be
# scored against answers produced under the old settings, so it freezes a
# second reference of its own. Naming only `baseline` here would have left that
# one untracked, which is the same as not freezing it at all.
.claude/hillclimb/*/*/*
!.claude/hillclimb/*/*/ref/
.superpowers/

# Throwaway working directory: one-off SQL run against a database by hand,
# scripts that drive a browser around someone else's product, and the
# screenshots they produce. None of it is a migration and none of it is a test —
# the migration a file here applied already lives in `supabase/migrations/`, and
# a copy of it that ran once on one machine is not a second source of truth.
scratchpad/

# Real wrangler configs carry account IDs — commit only the .example templates
worker/wrangler.toml
worker/wrangler.cron.toml

# Internal design docs never ship to the public repository
docs/superpowers/

# Competitor and market research. It is written here because this is where the
# agent is pointed, not because it belongs to the code — it belongs with the
# positioning docs, in their own repository. Publishing a teardown of a named
# company from the product's own public tree is a different act than writing it.
tasklet-ai-analysis.md

# MCP server configuration, written by whichever agent tool is being run in this
# checkout. It is not project configuration and it does not belong to anyone but
# the machine it was written on — but the reason it is named here is narrower
# than that, and it is the reason this line exists in the open repository and not
# the private one.
#
# The file that prompted it pointed a Supabase MCP server at a `project_ref`, and
# the ref was covan.app's production project. That identifier is deliberately
# absent from this tree: `0025_what_the_cache_already_paid_for.sql` carries its
# explanatory comments in the private copy and not in this one for exactly that
# reason, and `scripts/check-sync.sh` asserts the split every time it runs. All
# of which is undone by one `git add -A` in a checkout where the file is
# untracked and unignored, because an untracked file is not a protection — it is
# a file nobody has committed yet.
.mcp.json

# npm lockfiles. This project installs with bun — `bun.lock` and
# `worker/bun.lock` are the tracked ones, and CI runs
# `bun install --frozen-lockfile` against them. Anyone who reaches for
# `npm install` out of habit leaves a second lockfile describing the same tree
# by different rules, and `git add -A` will commit it without comment. The tool
# is not wrong; the artifact is just not ours to keep.
package-lock.json
